CHAPTERS = [
dict(title="An Evening in Ancient Athens", scene="acropolis", paras=[
"Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive. Tonight we are going to spend one gentle evening in Athens, about two and a half thousand years ago, when the great marble temple on the Acropolis was still new. There will be no battles tonight, and no speeches that matter. Only lamps being lit, supper being shared, and a city settling slowly into sleep.",
"Let yourself get comfortable. Let the pillow take the weight of your head, and let your jaw loosen a little. Breathe in slowly, as if you could smell warm stone and wild thyme after a long summer day. Then breathe out, longer than you breathed in, and feel the day begin to slip away behind you.",
"We arrive just as the sun goes down. A high rock rises above the rooftops, and along its top runs a row of pale columns, catching the last light. Below it, the houses of the city crowd together, roofed in red clay tiles, and here and there a doorway already glows with the small yellow flame of an oil lamp.",
"You do not need to remember any names tonight, or follow any story. We will simply wander, from the hillside to the market, into a family's courtyard, past a quiet dinner among friends, and up to the temple under the moon. If you drift away somewhere along the path, that is exactly as it should be.",
]),

dict(title="The Violet Hour", scene="pnyx", paras=[
"We are sitting on a low hill west of the Acropolis, on a broad shelf of grey rock worn smooth by thousands of feet. In the daytime, this is the Pnyx, where the citizens of Athens gather on assembly days to listen and to vote. This evening it is empty, except for us, a few tufts of dry grass, and the warm air rising from the stone.",
"To the east, beyond the city, there is a long mountain called Hymettus. As the sun sinks behind us, its whole slope begins to change colour, from gold to rose, and from rose to a deep, soft violet. Poets called Athens the violet-crowned city, and on an evening like this one, it is easy to see why.",
"Hymettus is famous for something else as well. On its slopes grow thyme and sage and savory, and the bees that visit them make a pale, fragrant honey that the Athenians prize above all others. Somewhere up there, in the dusk, rows of hives made of clay and woven straw are falling quiet, the last bees crawling home through their little doors.",
"Just below the southern side of the Acropolis, you can make out a great hollow in the hillside, a curve of seats cut into the slope. This is the theatre of the god Dionysus. In the spring festival, thousands of Athenians sit there from dawn till dusk, watching plays. Tonight its seats are empty and silver grey, and a hare is loping slowly across the circle of beaten earth where the chorus dances.",
"Below us, the city spreads out in every direction, a soft jumble of flat roofs and tiled roofs, narrow lanes and small courtyards. There are no tall towers here, apart from the rock itself. Most houses have only one floor, or two at the most, and their walls are made of sun-dried mud brick, plastered and washed in pale colours.",
"Far off to the south west, where the land runs down towards the sea, two long walls stretch away side by side, straight as ruled lines. They run for miles, all the way down to the harbour town of Piraeus, so that the road between the city and its ships is always protected. Tonight they are just two faint grey threads in the dusk.",
"At the end of them, the sea lies smooth and pale, and in the harbours a scatter of tiny lights is coming out, one by one. Those are the lamps of taverns along the waterfront, and of sailors sitting on the decks of merchant ships, and of the watchmen beside the long sheds where the warships rest, pulled up out of the water for the night.",
"Closer at hand, a goatherd is bringing his small flock down the next slope, the bells around their necks clinking softly as they pick their way between the rocks. He calls to them now and then, a low, wordless sound, and they answer with a bleat or two, and then the clinking moves away along the path and fades into the evening.",
"The first star appears over the shoulder of Hymettus, and then another. The rock beneath us still holds the warmth of the afternoon, like a stone taken from beside a fire. A little owl, small and round, with pale speckled feathers, lands on a boulder a few steps away, turns its head towards us, blinks its yellow eyes, and settles down to watch the night.",
]),

dict(title="The Agora at Lamplighting", scene="stoa", paras=[
"We walk down from the hill on a path of beaten earth, between low walls where lizards have stopped sunning themselves for the day. The path joins a wider street, and the street opens into a broad, level space, edged with long colonnades. This is the Agora, the heart of Athenian life, where people come to buy and sell, to argue, to gossip, and to idle away an hour in the shade.",
"At this time of evening, the market is closing. The sellers of fish have long since packed up their baskets, and the last of the vegetable sellers are lifting their trestles, folding their striped awnings, and tying their unsold onions and cabbages into cloth bundles. A boy sweeps up the leaves and straw from the beaten floor with a broom of bundled twigs.",
"Along the northern side runs a long, roofed colonnade with a solid wall at the back. Athenians call it the Painted Stoa, because inside, on great wooden panels, famous artists have painted scenes from the old stories and the city's past. In the half light, you can just make out the shapes of horses and ships, of warriors and gods, their colours softening into shadow.",
"In the daytime, people stroll beneath these columns to keep out of the sun, or sit on the steps with a friend. Philosophers will one day gather here to teach, and their followers will be named after the porch itself. But tonight, only an old man is left, sitting with his back against a column, a cloak around his shoulders, quietly watching the swallows dart overhead.",
"On the western side of the square stands a small round building with a cone shaped roof. This is the Tholos. Inside it, the members of the city's council who are on duty this month eat their meals together, at the city's expense. And every night, some of them sleep here too, so that the city is never without someone to answer a message in the dark.",
"Through the open door of the Tholos comes a soft glow, and the murmur of men talking over the remains of their supper. There is the smell of bread, and of cheese and olives, and of a little onion. Someone laughs quietly at a joke. A servant comes out with a jug and walks across to the fountain house to fill it with fresh water for the night.",
"At the corners of the streets, and outside many doorways, stand square stone pillars with a carved head on top, bearded and calm. These are herms, and they belong to the god Hermes, protector of travellers and of boundaries. As people pass them, some touch them lightly, for luck. One has a small wreath of leaves hung around its neck, already beginning to wilt.",
"High in the plane trees along the edge of the square, the cicadas, which have sung all through the hot afternoon, are finally slowing down. One stops, and then another, until only a single cicada is still rasping away, slower and slower, like a clock running down. And then it, too, falls silent, and the evening becomes very quiet indeed.",
"Near the lawcourts, on a small stone stand, there is a water clock, a clay jar with a little bronze spout near the bottom. In the daytime it is used to time the speeches in court, each speaker allowed only as long as it takes the water to run out. Tonight it stands empty and dry, and no one is in a hurry to be anywhere at all.",
"A slave from one of the public offices walks slowly along the colonnade with a taper, lighting the lamps that hang on chains between the columns. Each small clay lamp holds a pool of olive oil and a twist of linen for a wick. One by one, the flames come up, small and steady, and the long row of columns glows softly against the deepening blue.",
]),

dict(title="A House Below the Hill", scene="courtyard", paras=[
"Let us follow a narrow lane away from the square, uphill a little, towards the houses that cluster under the rocky slopes. The lane is just wide enough for a donkey with baskets. Its walls are blank and plain, broken only by wooden doors, and above them, now and then, a small high window shows a square of lamplight.",
"We stop at one door, painted a faded blue, with a little stone herm standing beside it. The door is fastened with a wooden bar on the inside, but it has been left open for the evening air. A worn stone sill lies across the threshold, hollowed in the middle by many years of feet, and a scent of cooking drifts out to meet us.",
"Inside, a short passage leads into a courtyard, open to the sky. This is the centre of an Athenian home. All the rooms of the house open onto it, the storerooms and the kitchen, the bedrooms and the room for guests, so that the family lives looking inward, around this small square of beaten earth and sky.",
"In the middle of the courtyard stands a small stone altar, about knee high, dedicated to Zeus of the Courtyard, the guardian of the household. A few grains of incense smoulder on top, sending up a thin grey thread of sweet smoke that rises straight and slow into the still air, and then spreads and vanishes above the rooftop.",
"In one corner there is a well, with a round stone mouth and a rope hanging from a wooden beam. Beside it stands a tall clay water jar, with three handles, two at the sides for lifting and one at the back for pouring. Its surface is cool and slightly damp to the touch, and a little frog sits at its foot, perfectly still.",
"Along one side of the courtyard runs a narrow porch, with a few wooden posts holding up a tiled roof. Beneath it stands an upright loom, almost as tall as a person, with its threads hanging down in long, even rows, weighted at the bottom by little clay pyramids that sway and click softly whenever the cloth is touched.",
"The mistress of the house has been weaving here through the afternoon, a length of fine wool cloth in a soft madder red, with a narrow band of pattern along the edge. Now she has put down her shuttle for the day. The half finished cloth hangs quiet on the loom, and her basket of wool sits beside it, spun into neat balls.",
"Above the storerooms, a wooden ladder leads up to a second floor, where the family sleep in two small rooms under the roof. Their windows are small and high, with wooden shutters, and one has been propped open to let in the cooler evening air. From up there, if you stood on tiptoe, you could just see the columns of the temple over the neighbouring rooftops.",
"A fig tree grows against the far wall, its broad, rough leaves spreading over the corner like a canopy. A few ripe figs hang among them, purple and soft, and one has fallen and split open on the ground. The tree has been there longer than anyone in the house can remember, and its roots have lifted the edge of the paving stones.",
"Against the walls of the storeroom stand tall clay jars, some as high as your waist, for olive oil, wine, barley and dried figs. Their mouths are sealed with plugs of clay or covered with flat stones. In the warm air, the whole house smells faintly of oil and earth, of wool and smoke, and of the figs ripening on the tree.",
]),

dict(title="Supper in the Courtyard", scene="courtyard", paras=[
"In the kitchen, a small room at the corner of the courtyard, a cooking pot sits over a portable clay brazier filled with glowing charcoal. The smoke drifts out through a gap in the roof tiles. An older woman of the household stirs the pot with a wooden spoon, tastes it, adds a pinch of salt, and nods to herself.",
"Supper tonight is simple, as most Athenian meals are. There are flat cakes of barley, kneaded with water and a little oil, and a few loaves of wheat bread bought from the baker. There are olives, black and green, in a small bowl. There is a soft white cheese of goat's milk, and a dish of lentils cooked slowly with onion and thyme.",
"There is a small fish too, grilled over the charcoal and laid on a dish with a drizzle of oil and a squeeze of something sharp. The Athenians love fish, and the market has plenty of it, sardines and anchovies and little red mullet brought up from the harbour every morning. Meat is for festival days, when there is a sacrifice and everyone shares.",
"The family eat together on stools and benches under the porch, with the lamp set on a little table between them. The father talks about the price of olive oil, and about a neighbour's new mule. The children are hungry and eat quickly, dipping their bread in the oil, and their mother tells them gently to slow down.",
"Afterwards, there are figs from the tree, and a few almonds, and a small piece of honeycomb from Hymettus, dripping golden onto a plate. The children share it carefully, licking their fingers. The grandmother takes only a little, and closes her eyes as she tastes it, the way people do when a flavour reminds them of something long ago.",
"The lamp they eat by is a small dish of red clay, shaped like a flattened bowl with a spout at one end. It holds olive oil, and the wick rests in the spout. Its flame is no bigger than a fingertip, and it gives a soft, warm light that does not reach very far. The corners of the courtyard sink into shadow, and the sky above grows deep.",
"When the meal is done, the children are sent to wash their hands and faces in a basin by the well. The water is cold, and they gasp and laugh. Then the grandmother sits down on a low stool beneath the fig tree, and the youngest climbs into her lap, and she begins to tell a story, in a slow, low voice.",
"It is one of the old tales that every Athenian child knows. The story goes that long ago, two gods wished to be the patron of this city, and each offered it a gift. One struck the rock and brought forth a spring of salt water. The other planted the first olive tree. And the people chose the olive, and so the city has belonged to Athena ever since.",
"The child is already half asleep, her head heavy against her grandmother's shoulder. The story slows, and pauses, and goes on a little more, and pauses again. Above them, a bat flickers across the open square of sky and is gone. The grandmother hums softly, an old tune without words, and rocks gently on her stool.",
]),

dict(title="Friends After Dinner", scene="andron", paras=[
"On the other side of the lane, in a neighbour's house, a small dinner among friends is going on. The room where it takes place is called the andron, the men's room. It is square, with a raised edge of plaster running all around the walls, and on that raised edge stand wooden couches, seven of them, each with a mattress and soft cushions.",
"The guests have washed their hands and taken off their sandals at the door, and now they recline on the couches, leaning on their left elbows, their cloaks loosely draped around them. In front of each couch stands a small low table, and on the tables are the remains of the meal, crusts of bread, a few olive stones, a scatter of nutshells.",
"At the start of the drinking, before anything else, the host poured a few drops of unmixed wine onto the floor, as an offering to the gods, and everyone sang a short hymn together. Now each guest wears a garland on his head, woven of myrtle and ivy, and the leaves give off a fresh green scent that mingles with the lamp smoke.",
"In the middle of the room stands a large bowl of painted clay on a three-legged stand. This is the krater, the mixing bowl. In it, wine is mixed with water, usually far more water than wine, for to drink wine unmixed is thought rude and rather foolish. A boy with a jug moves quietly from couch to couch, filling the shallow cups.",
"The cups are wide and flat, on slender stems, painted black and red. When one is tipped back to drink, a little picture appears at the bottom, a dolphin perhaps, or a dancing figure, or a running hare. One of the guests smiles at the hare in the bottom of his cup, and turns it to show his neighbour.",
"Tonight the gathering is a gentle one. The host has chosen a sensible man to decide how strong the wine will be, and how many bowls will be mixed, and he has decided on only a few, and well watered. The talk drifts easily, from a play that some of them saw at the theatre in the spring, to the vines on a cousin's farm, to the meaning of a dream.",
"After a while, someone asks for the lyre. It is passed from hand to hand until it reaches a guest who can play, a quiet man with grey in his beard. He tunes the strings slowly, one by one, then begins a simple song, and the others listen with their eyes half closed, and one or two of them join in on the last line of each verse.",
"Along the walls, the light comes from lamps set on tall bronze stands, each with a little dish of oil at the top. The flames lean and straighten whenever someone moves. Painted on the plaster, just below the ceiling, runs a band of red and black, a pattern of interlocking lines and leaves that seems to wander on and on around the room.",
"Later, the talk grows slower. Someone sets a riddle, and the others think about it for a long time and cannot solve it, and nobody minds. One guest has quietly fallen asleep on his couch, his cup still resting in his hand, and the boy with the jug gently takes it from him and sets it down on the table without a sound.",
"At last the host rises and thanks his friends, and they begin to leave, one by one, putting on their sandals at the door. Outside, each is met by a servant with a small torch, or a lantern of horn, to light the way home through the dark lanes. Their footsteps and low voices wander off in different directions, and the lane falls quiet again.",
"In the empty room, the boy blows out the lamps on their tall stands, one after another, until only one is left. He gathers the cups and stacks them in a basket, and covers the mixing bowl with a cloth. Then he too lies down, on a mat in the corner of the courtyard, and pulls his cloak over his shoulders, and is asleep almost at once.",
]),

dict(title="Up to the Temple", scene="acropolis", paras=[
"Now let us leave the houses behind and climb up towards the rock itself. The way winds up the western slope in long, easy turns, paved in places, worn smooth by the feet of processions. The moon has risen now, almost full, and the path is pale and clear in front of us, and our shadows walk beside us on the stones.",
"As we climb, the city falls away below us, and the air grows a little cooler and fresher. We pass a small spring in a cave at the foot of the rock, where water drips steadily into a stone basin, and a few clay cups have been left on the ledge for anyone who is thirsty. The water is cold and tastes faintly of stone.",
"At the top of the path, we come to the great gateway, a broad stairway of marble leading up between rows of columns. By day, people pass through here in a steady stream, carrying offerings, leading animals, chattering. Tonight there is only a single guard, sitting on a step with his spear leaning beside him, who nods to us without a word.",
"Beyond the gateway, the top of the rock opens out like a wide, uneven plateau, crowded with shrines and statues and little altars. And there, ahead of us and to the right, stands the temple of Athena, the Parthenon, larger than anything else in the city, its rows of columns rising pale and still in the moonlight.",
"The marble came from a mountain called Pentelikon, a few hours' walk to the north east, and it was dragged here block by block on heavy sledges and ox carts. When it is new, as it is now, it is almost white, with the faintest warm tint, like honey stirred into milk. In the moonlight it seems to glow from within.",
"Up close, the columns are enormous, each made of many drums of stone stacked one upon another, so smoothly fitted that the joins are almost invisible. They are not quite straight. Each one swells very slightly in the middle, and leans very slightly inwards, so gently that the eye cannot see it, and yet the whole temple looks alive.",
"High above the columns, around the outside of the building, runs a band of carved figures, and inside the colonnade there is another, long and narrow, showing a great procession, riders and walkers, musicians and elders, young women carrying bowls, all moving the same way. In daylight they are painted in soft colours. In the moonlight, they are only shapes, walking quietly on forever.",
"Inside the temple, in the dark, stands a great statue of Athena, many times the height of a person, made of ivory and gold on a frame of wood. Her face and arms are ivory, her robe and armour gold. No lamp is lit before her tonight. The great doors are closed, and the goddess stands alone in the darkness, keeping her own silence.",
"Out in the open, between the temple and the gateway, stands another Athena, made of bronze, so tall that her helmet and the tip of her spear rise above the rooftops. It is said that sailors coming home round the southern cape can see the glint of that spear point from far out at sea, and know they are nearly home.",
"From the northern edge of the rock, the whole plain of Attica lies open beneath the moon. There are dark hills on three sides, and olive groves in long grey rows, and the white threads of roads leading out to the villages. And to the south west, beyond the Long Walls, the sea shines like a sheet of beaten silver, all the way to the islands.",
"Not far away, inside a little walled enclosure, grows an olive tree, old and gnarled and silver leaved. People say it is the very tree that Athena planted, the gift that won her the city. Its leaves stir faintly in the night air, showing their pale undersides to the moon, and then turn dark again.",
"On a ledge high on the temple, half hidden in the shadow of the carved stone, a pair of little owls have made their home. They are Athena's birds, and nobody would dream of disturbing them. Every now and then one of them calls, a soft, rising whistle, and the other answers from somewhere above, and the sound drifts out over the sleeping city.",
]),

dict(title="The City Sleeps", scene="pnyx", paras=[
"From the edge of the rock, we look down over Athens by night. Most of the lamps have gone out now. Here and there, a single doorway still glows, where someone is sitting up late, or a baby is being fed, or a baker has already started the dough for the morning. Otherwise, the city is a soft grey sea of roofs under the moon.",
"Down in the Agora, the Tholos is still faintly lit. Inside, the councillors on duty for the night are lying on simple beds along the curved wall, wrapped in their cloaks. One of them is awake, sitting by the lamp, in case a messenger should come. But no messenger is coming tonight. Everything is quiet, and he lets his head nod.",
"Along the walls of the city, the watchmen walk their slow rounds, from tower to tower, their sandals scuffing softly on the stone. They pause at the corners to look out over the dark fields and olive groves beyond, where nothing moves except the leaves. Then they turn and walk back again, and the night goes on, unhurried.",
"In a little potter's workshop near the city gate, the kiln that was fired this afternoon is slowly cooling. Inside it, stacked carefully, are new cups and jugs and lamps, still too hot to touch. The potter has gone home to his supper, and the workshop is dark, except for a faint red glow through the cracks around the kiln's door.",
"Far away, down at the harbour, the ships are resting too. The great warships lie in their long roofed sheds, drawn up on stone slipways, their oars stacked, their hulls black with pitch. The merchant ships sit at anchor in the bay, rocking gently, lanterns at their sterns, their crews asleep on deck under the warm sky.",
"Overhead, the stars are thick and bright, and the Athenians know many of them by name and story. There is the hunter Orion, not yet risen, who will climb over the mountains before dawn. There is the little cluster of the Pleiades, the seven sisters, whose rising in early summer tells farmers it is time to harvest, and whose setting in the autumn warns sailors to bring their ships home.",
"In the houses below, the families are sleeping on low beds with wooden frames and mattresses stuffed with wool. On the hottest nights, some carry their bedding up onto the flat roofs and sleep beneath the open sky, with only a light blanket. The grandmother from the courtyard is asleep at last, and the child is asleep beside her.",
"Out on the hillsides, beyond the city walls, the olive groves stand in long silver rows, their leaves just stirring. Somewhere among them, a dog barks once, and then thinks better of it, and lies down again. A breeze comes up from the sea, very faint, carrying the smell of salt and pine resin and warm dry earth.",
"In a stable at the edge of town, a donkey shifts its weight from one foot to the other and sighs, a long, deep sigh, the way donkeys do. In the yard outside, a few hens have tucked their heads under their wings. And on a rooftop nearby, a cat sits perfectly still, watching the moon, its tail curled neatly around its paws.",
"The owl on the temple calls one more time, a soft, falling note that floats out over the rooftops. And then there is no sound at all, except the leaves, and the far, faint breathing of the sea, and your own slow breath, in, and out, as Athens sleeps around you beneath the moon.",
]),

dict(title="Goodnight, Athens", scene="acropolis", paras=[
"You can rest now, like the family in the courtyard, like the friends who drifted off on their couches, like the councillor nodding by his lamp. There is nothing you need to carry tonight, no market to open, no loom to finish, no message to wait for. All of that can wait until morning, and morning is a long way off.",
"Let your breathing slow, the way the cicadas slowed at dusk. Let your hands grow heavy, the way the loom weights hang quiet at the end of their threads. And let your thoughts drift out and away, like the thin thread of incense from the little altar, rising slowly, spreading, and fading into the night air.",
"High on its rock, the temple stands pale and still under the moon, as it will tomorrow night, and the night after, for a very long time. The olive tree stirs its silver leaves. The owls keep their quiet watch. And below them, the violet-crowned city sleeps, safe and warm, all the way down to the sea.",
"Thank you for spending this evening in Athens with The Quiet Archive. The lamps are out, the stories have been told, and the city is dreaming. Sleep well. Goodnight.",
]),
]
